{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_vvqhyu5u46ui56ff2bd4wc4g6xst5aqt42hmuieutqcue73fsc4a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single function, obfuscated by C **digraphs** (`%:`=`#`, `<%`=`{`, `%>`=`}`) and **`<iso646.h>`** spellings (`bitor`=`|`, `compl`=`~`). Confirmed with `gcc -E`:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x, int y) { int result = x | ~y; printf(\"%d\", result); puts(\"\"); }\n```\n\nThe existing Rust translation was **already correct** — I found no behavioural divergence and changed no logic in `src/lib.rs`.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n| file | contents |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | `nm -D` inventory. C exports exactly `driver`; Rust exports exactly `driver`. Diff empty; nothing untranslated, no stubs. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 13 rows. Mechanical grep proved the C has **0** rejection branches (no `return`, `assert`, null/range check, enum, or pointer), so every row is a *must-not-reject* row covering the mandated boundary classes. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 8 derived axes → 29 rows (operand classes, every bit position, every decimal output width, boundary cross-products, call sequencing, stdio buffering modes, sink shapes). |\n\n## Phases B–D — 46 differential tests, 5 binaries\n\nBoth `.so`s are `dlopen`ed via `libloading` and called only through the exported `driver` symbol; output is compared by redirecting fd 1 and diffing bytes. All 29 `CONFIGS.md` rows and all `ERRORS.md` rows pass, under both `default` and `--no-default-features` (the crate declares no features — `scripts/verify.sh` enumerates them from `cargo metadata` rather than assuming).\n\n## Two harness bugs I had to fix — the second one mattered\n\n1. **libtest contamination**: libtest's own progress output was leaking into my fd-1 captures. Fixed by flushing Rust-side buffers before each capture window and pinning `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1`.\n\n2. **Stale artifact made the whole suite vacuous.** `cargo test` does *not* emit a `cdylib` (it isn't linkable by an integration test), so the harness was loading a `.so` left behind by an earlier `cargo build`. I caught this only by running a **negative control**: I injected `x ^ !y` in place of `x | !y` and *all 45 tests still passed*. The harness now builds the cdylib itself into a separate target dir, asserts it is newer than `src/lib.rs`, and `d5_harness_loads_two_distinct_objects` uses `dladdr` to prove the two function pointers come from different files.\n\nI then re-validated sensitivity with four mutations (all reverted; `src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to its pre-testing state):\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| `x \\| !y` → `x ^ !y` | 21/29 Phase B rows |\n| `\"%d\"` → `\"%u\"` | 23/29 |\n| dropped the `puts(\"\")` newline | 29/29 |\n| spurious `INT_MIN` rejection | 9 Phase B + 8 Phase C rows |\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: LLVM rewrites the Rust `puts(\"\")` into `putchar('\\n')`, so the import lists differ (`puts` vs `putchar`). I treated this as acceptable rather than forcing the symbol, since the *exported* surface is identical and the emitted byte stream is provably the same — GCC performs the same rewrite. It's documented in `SYMBOLS.md`.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (source checksums verified unchanged; only the prescribed `build/` directory was added). Re-run everything with `cd translation && ./scripts/verify.sh`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1016,"cache_read_input_tokens":113822,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1016,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c785e59e-e81c-4bb4-833c-7896b151d93a","uuid":"e6adcaf1-3814-469d-b5d1-c8fbc38db580","timestamp":"2026-08-28T17:31:37.812Z"}
